From ee0034edac28be44742f5fa5922e541c00d61e52 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Season Tempo Build <claude@seasontempo.local>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 22:04:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Player dues: who owes, how much, and what a budget line means
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three faults, one workflow.

TEAM TOTAL WAS WRITTEN PER PLAYER.
  A coach typed 48000 into a field labelled 'Total due for the season' for a
  14-player team and got 14 obligations of $48,000 — a season total of
  $672,000, fourteen times what was meant. The server had always been
  per-player; the label was wrong, and one field carried two meanings.

  Now the mode is explicit, and money is split in integer cents. Never
  floating-point division: a naive split of $48,000 across 14 collects
  $47,999.98 or $48,000.14, and the gap is real money a family is short or
  over. The remainder is handed out one cent at a time over a stable order, so
  sum(obligations) equals the entered total exactly.

DUES COULD BE SET BUT NOT CORRECTED.
  The bulk flow only ever created, so a coach who set the wrong amount was told
  'Set dues for 0 players' — true, and useless. Editing is now possible while
  nothing has been paid, and refuses whole rather than in part once it has:
  reallocating a total when some families have already paid produces a number
  that is no longer the total anyone entered.

NOT EVERY PLAYER OWES DUES.
  A team total is not the roster divided by headcount. Exemption is now stated
  in its own column rather than inferred: deleting the obligation reads as an
  oversight, and $0 reads as 'Paid in Full', which claims money was received.
  Reversible, and excluded from every allocation and total.

Also: a budget line is a plan, not a charge. Creating one now says so, offers
the dues handoff, and synchronises nothing afterwards.

Tournament creation appeared to fail and was succeeding every time — five
attempts in production created five tournaments. The drawer is resolved by
looking the new id up in the server-rendered rows, and the create path was the
one mutation here that never refreshed, so nothing on screen changed.

Includes the approved Saved-on-create fix: a resource you create is yours
immediately, recorded as a relationship rather than inferred from
created_by_organization_id, which the importer also sets.
